#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
"""Score the reader load of Python source files, one function at a time.

WHAT THIS IS. A panel of per-function measures that each capture one thing a
reader has to hold in mind, with the widely used thresholds for the ones that
have them, and a coarse band per function. It is NOT a single readability
number: the measures agree at the extremes and disagree in the middle, and a
weighted blend (the Maintainability Index mistake) hides which one fired.
Read the flags, then the function.

THE MEASURES, and where each comes from.

* ``loc``        Logical code lines in the function's span: non-blank,
                 non-comment, NOT the docstring, which is reported beside it
                 as ``doc``, and NOT a line whose first token only continues
                 a string or closes a bracket (the lines of a wrapped message,
                 a string-keyed literal or a wrapped call). Those are read,
                 not reasoned about: every loc flag on the middle-heavy
                 panel sample rested on them. The physical count is in the
                 JSON (``loc_physical``). The strongest single predictor of
                 reading time and defect density; most of McCabe's apparent
                 power is this measure in a costume. A docstring helps the
                 reader, so it is shown and never charged. Threshold 50
                 (``--max-loc``).
* ``mccabe``     McCabe cyclomatic complexity as hypergumbo's Python analyzer
                 stamps it on the symbol (``Symbol.cyclomatic_complexity``):
                 1 + if/elif, loops, except handlers, ``and``/``or`` operands
                 beyond the first, ternaries, comprehension ``if`` clauses and
                 match cases. It counts paths to TEST, not effort to read.
                 Threshold 10 (``--max-mccabe``), the classic.
* ``cognitive``  Cognitive complexity after SonarSource's 2018 specification,
                 computed here on the tree-sitter parse: +1 for each ``if``,
                 ``for``, ``while``, ``except``, ternary and ``match``, PLUS
                 the current nesting level for each; +1 flat for each ``elif``
                 and ``else`` (including a loop's or a ``try``'s ``else``);
                 +1 for each run of one boolean operator (``a and b and c`` is
                 one, ``a and b or c`` is two; parentheses start a new run);
                 +1 for each direct recursive call: a call by bare name for
                 a function, ``self.``/``cls.`` plus the name for a method
                 (``other.rules()`` inside ``rules`` is somebody else's
                 function). Nesting rises inside the bodies of those
                 constructs and inside nested ``def``s and lambdas. Comprehensions, ``with``, ``try``/``finally`` and
                 ``assert`` count nothing, as in the specification. Threshold
                 15 (``--max-cognitive``), Sonar's default.
* ``nesting``    Deepest statement nesting in the body: ``if`` (one level for
                 the whole if/elif/else chain), loops, ``try``, ``with``,
                 ``match`` and each ``case``, nested ``def`` and ``class``.
                 The thing McCabe cannot see. Threshold 4 (``--max-nesting``),
                 the common linter default.
* ``dataflow``   The number of DISTINCT definition-to-use edges, as
                 (variable, definition line, use line) triples, in the
                 function's data dependence graph, built with hypergumbo's
                 own CFG and reaching-definitions solver
                 (``cfg.build_function_cfg``, ``populate_def_use_for_cfg``,
                 ``solve_reaching_defs``, ``late_capture_edges``, the
                 parameters as entry definitions exactly as ``ddg_build``
                 hands them in). This is the dependency-degree measure with
                 the best comprehension evidence: it tracked measured
                 cognitive load in the 2021 brain-imaging study where McCabe
                 did not. The raw edge list can repeat a triple where a nested
                 function reads a captured variable (each read is keyed to the
                 nested ``def`` line as well), so triples are counted once.
                 There is no widely recognised threshold, so none is applied
                 by default (``--max-dataflow`` sets one); sort on it with
                 ``--sort dataflow``. ``vars`` is the distinct variables those
                 edges carry.
                 A trailing ``?`` on the cell (``dataflow_uncovered`` in the
                 JSON) means hypergumbo's own coverage gate
                 (``cfg.uncovered_semantic_lines``) found call sites the
                 CFG's recorded statements do not reach: the def/use pass
                 never saw part of the body, so the count is a FLOOR, and the
                 taint walk forfeits refutation over the same function. The
                 measure under-counts where the def/use extractor does not
                 model a construct (INV-lupav); it does not over-count. A
                 function the solver bails out of shows ``-``.
* ``reach``      The farthest a reader has to look back: the most code lines
                 (as ``loc`` counts them) lying strictly between a definition
                 made IN THE BODY and a use that reads it. Parameters are left
                 out, because the signature shows them and nobody scrolls
                 back for one; counting them made this column the function's
                 length plus its docstring. A same-line or adjacent-line
                 dependency reads 0.
* ``gloc``       Code lines of the same-file module-level bindings the
                 function reads: names bound at module level by assignment,
                 ``def`` or ``class`` (imports are not counted) that appear in
                 the body and are not rebound there. A data binding (a regex,
                 a table, a constant) weighs its code lines, string lines
                 included, because its text is what the reader decodes; a
                 ``def`` or ``class`` weighs one line, because a caller reads
                 a name and a signature, not a body (charging bodies made the
                 two easiest functions of a file the highest on this column).
                 A cheap proxy for the non-local reasoning no intra-function
                 measure sees: a function that decodes three module-level
                 regexes spanning thirty lines reads 30, where a count of
                 names reads 3 and puts a one-line regex level with a
                 twelve-line one. The JSON carries the count (``globals``),
                 the names (``global_names``) and the lines
                 (``global_lines``). No threshold; ``--sort globals`` orders
                 by the lines.
* ``params``     JSON only: parameters, from the analyzer's
                 ``meta["parameters"]``; ``null`` when it stamped none.
* ``calls``      JSON only: call expressions in the body (fan-out by
                 spelling, not by resolution: the single-file analysis cannot
                 resolve what is defined elsewhere). Neither of these two
                 separated easy functions from hard ones on any panel sample,
                 so the text table leaves them out.

THE BAND. ``ok`` = no threshold exceeded, ``watch`` = one, ``split`` = two or
more. A flag names the measure, its value and the threshold it crossed
(``cognitive 16>15``), so a one-point margin is visible. ``--strict`` exits 1
when any function is not ``ok``, so the script can gate. A suspect
``dataflow`` never changes a band; it is a warning that the cell is a floor.

THE FILE SUMMARY also reports ``module_lines``, the code lines outside every
function and class, and ``module_bindings``, the module-level names, because
a file's difficulty may live there (dense regexes, lookup tables) and no
function row shows it.

WHAT IT DOES NOT SEE. Naming, comments, non-local reasoning beyond the
``gloc`` lines (what a callee does, what an imported collaborator means),
polymorphic dispatch, exceptions thrown from elsewhere, async continuations:
every other measure here is intra-function. Module-level code is counted as
lines only. KNOWN MEASURE GAP: cursor and offset arithmetic. A short loop that
rewrites a few index variables (``j += w; i = j + 1; j += 1``) scores 0 on
every thresholded measure, and the middle-heavy panel's one miss, rated 4 by
all three judges, was exactly that shape at cognitive 14 and mccabe 9. Only
``dataflow`` relative to ``loc`` hints at it (52 edges on 22 lines), and it
carries no threshold. No threshold change catches it without false alarms
elsewhere; it waits for a measure, not a recalibration.

USAGE::

    scripts/score-readability FILE.py [FILE.py ...] [--sort cognitive|mccabe|
        nesting|loc|dataflow|reach|globals|line|name] [--top N] [--json]
        [--strict] [--max-loc 50 --max-mccabe 10 --max-cognitive 15
         --max-nesting 4 --max-dataflow N] [--no-dataflow]

Runs on the installed hypergumbo (the editable checkout in this repo).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import io
import json
import statistics
import sys
import tokenize
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Optional

_INCREMENT_AND_NEST = frozenset({
    "if_statement", "for_statement", "while_statement", "except_clause",
    "conditional_expression", "match_statement",
})
_INCREMENT_FLAT = frozenset({"elif_clause", "else_clause"})
# A ``block`` directly under one of these is one nesting level deeper for the
# cognitive walk (the if/elif/else chain shares the if's level; a ``case``
# body is the match's one level).
_NEST_BLOCK_OWNERS = frozenset({
    "if_statement", "elif_clause", "else_clause", "for_statement",
    "while_statement", "except_clause", "case_clause", "function_definition",
})
# Statement nesting for ``nesting``: one level per construct.
_NESTING_STATEMENTS = frozenset({
    "if_statement", "for_statement", "while_statement", "try_statement",
    "with_statement", "match_statement", "case_clause", "function_definition",
    "class_definition",
})
_DEFINITIONS = frozenset({"function_definition", "class_definition"})
_IMPORTS = frozenset({"import_statement", "import_from_statement", "future_import_statement"})
_ASSIGNMENTS = frozenset({"assignment", "augmented_assignment"})
# A line whose FIRST token is one of these is read, not reasoned about.
_CLOSERS = frozenset({")", "]", "}"})
_STRING_TOKENS = frozenset({tokenize.STRING, getattr(tokenize, "FSTRING_START", -1)})
_SKIPPED_TOKENS = frozenset({
    tokenize.ENCODING, tokenize.NL, tokenize.NEWLINE, tokenize.INDENT, tokenize.DEDENT,
    tokenize.COMMENT,
})
_SORT_KEYS = (
    "cognitive", "mccabe", "nesting", "loc", "dataflow", "reach", "globals", "line", "name",
)


@dataclass
class FunctionScore:
    """One function's panel. ``None`` = the producer could not say."""

    name: str
    start_line: int
    end_line: int
    loc: int
    loc_physical: int
    doc: int
    mccabe: Optional[int]
    cognitive: int
    nesting: int
    dataflow: Optional[int]
    dataflow_uncovered: Optional[int]
    vars: Optional[int]
    reach: Optional[int]
    globals: int
    global_names: list[str]
    global_lines: int
    params: Optional[int]
    calls: int
    flags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    band: str = "ok"

    @property
    def dataflow_suspect(self) -> bool:
        """Measured, and the coverage gate found unreached call sites (or could not say)."""
        return self.dataflow is not None and (
            self.dataflow_uncovered is None or self.dataflow_uncovered > 0
        )


@dataclass
class FileScore:
    path: str
    lines: int
    functions: list[FunctionScore]
    summary: dict[str, Any]


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Thresholds:
    loc: int = 50
    mccabe: int = 10
    cognitive: int = 15
    nesting: int = 4
    dataflow: Optional[int] = None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- parsing

def _parse(source: bytes) -> Any:
    import tree_sitter
    from tree_sitter_language_pack import get_language

    return tree_sitter.Parser(get_language("python")).parse(source).root_node


def _text(node: Any, source: bytes) -> str:
    return source[node.start_byte:node.end_byte].decode("utf-8", errors="replace")


def _qualified_name(node: Any, source: bytes) -> str:
    """``Class.method`` / ``outer.inner`` from the enclosing definitions."""
    parts: list[str] = []
    current = node
    while current is not None:
        if current.type in _DEFINITIONS:
            name = current.child_by_field_name("name")
            parts.append(_text(name, source) if name is not None else "<anonymous>")
        current = current.parent
    return ".".join(reversed(parts))


def _function_nodes(root: Any) -> list[Any]:
    """Every ``function_definition`` in source order, nested ones included."""
    found: list[Any] = []
    stack = [root]
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type == "function_definition":
            found.append(node)
        stack.extend(reversed(node.children))
    return found


def _docstring_span(fn_node: Any) -> Optional[tuple[int, int]]:
    """1-based inclusive line span of the body's leading docstring, if any."""
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    if body is None or body.named_child_count == 0:
        return None
    # tree-sitter-python puts a block's leading docstring ``string`` directly
    # under the block; an ``expression_statement`` wrapper is accepted too.
    first = body.named_children[0]
    if first.type == "expression_statement" and first.named_child_count == 1:
        first = first.named_children[0]
    if first.type != "string":
        return None
    return first.start_point[0] + 1, first.end_point[0] + 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- measures

def code_lines(
    lines: list[str], start_line: int, end_line: int, doc_span: Optional[tuple[int, int]] = None,
) -> set[int]:
    """The 1-based line numbers in the span that hold code: non-blank, non-comment, not docstring."""
    out: set[int] = set()
    for number in range(start_line, end_line + 1):
        if doc_span is not None and doc_span[0] <= number <= doc_span[1]:
            continue
        stripped = lines[number - 1].strip() if number - 1 < len(lines) else ""
        if stripped and not stripped.startswith("#"):
            out.add(number)
    return out


def non_logical_lines(source: bytes) -> set[int]:
    """Lines whose first token only continues a string or closes a bracket.

    Tokenised once per file. A file the tokenizer gives up on part-way (an
    unclosed bracket at the end) keeps the lines classified before that point.
    """
    first: dict[int, tuple[int, str]] = {}
    try:
        for tok in tokenize.tokenize(io.BytesIO(source).readline):
            if tok.type not in _SKIPPED_TOKENS:
                first.setdefault(tok.start[0], (tok.type, tok.string))
    except (tokenize.TokenError, SyntaxError):
        pass
    return {
        line for line, (ttype, text) in first.items()
        if ttype in _STRING_TOKENS or (ttype == tokenize.OP and text in _CLOSERS)
    }


def _is_method(fn_node: Any) -> bool:
    """True when the function's enclosing block is a class body (decorators looked through)."""
    parent = fn_node.parent
    if parent is not None and parent.type == "decorated_definition":
        parent = parent.parent
    return (
        parent is not None and parent.type == "block"
        and parent.parent is not None and parent.parent.type == "class_definition"
    )


def _is_recursive_call(call: Any, simple_name: str, source: bytes, is_method: bool) -> bool:
    """A bare-name call for a function; ``self.``/``cls.`` plus the name for a method."""
    fn = call.child_by_field_name("function")
    if fn is None:
        return False
    if not is_method:
        return fn.type == "identifier" and _text(fn, source) == simple_name
    if fn.type != "attribute":
        return False
    receiver = fn.child_by_field_name("object")
    attr = fn.child_by_field_name("attribute")
    return (
        receiver is not None and receiver.type == "identifier"
        and _text(receiver, source) in ("self", "cls")
        and attr is not None and _text(attr, source) == simple_name
    )


def cognitive_complexity(fn_node: Any, source: bytes) -> int:
    """SonarSource cognitive complexity of one function body; see the module docstring."""
    name_node = fn_node.child_by_field_name("name")
    simple_name = _text(name_node, source) if name_node is not None else ""
    is_method = _is_method(fn_node)
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    if body is None:
        return 0
    total = 0

    def visit(node: Any, nesting: int) -> None:
        nonlocal total
        kind = node.type
        if kind in _INCREMENT_AND_NEST:
            total += 1 + nesting
        elif kind in _INCREMENT_FLAT:
            total += 1
        elif kind == "boolean_operator":
            parent = node.parent
            op = node.child_by_field_name("operator")
            same_run = (
                parent is not None and parent.type == "boolean_operator"
                and op is not None
                and _text(parent.child_by_field_name("operator"), source) == _text(op, source)
            )
            if not same_run:
                total += 1
        elif kind == "call" and _is_recursive_call(node, simple_name, source, is_method):
            total += 1
        for child in node.children:
            child_nesting = nesting
            if child.type == "block" and kind in _NEST_BLOCK_OWNERS:
                child_nesting += 1
            elif kind in ("conditional_expression", "lambda") and child.is_named:
                child_nesting += 1
            visit(child, child_nesting)

    for child in body.children:
        visit(child, 0)
    return total


def max_nesting(fn_node: Any) -> int:
    """Deepest statement nesting inside the body (the body itself is depth 0)."""
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    if body is None:
        return 0
    deepest = 0

    def visit(node: Any, depth: int) -> None:
        nonlocal deepest
        if node.type in _NESTING_STATEMENTS:
            depth += 1
            deepest = max(deepest, depth)
        for child in node.children:
            visit(child, depth)

    for child in body.children:
        visit(child, 0)
    return deepest


def count_calls(fn_node: Any) -> int:
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    if body is None:
        return 0
    total = 0
    stack = [body]
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type == "call":
            total += 1
        stack.extend(node.children)
    return total


def _target_names(node: Any, source: bytes) -> set[str]:
    """Every identifier inside an assignment target (a name, a tuple, a list, a star)."""
    names: set[str] = set()
    stack = [node]
    while stack:
        current = stack.pop()
        if current.type == "identifier":
            names.add(_text(current, source))
        elif current.type in ("attribute", "subscript"):
            continue  # ``x.y = v`` / ``x[i] = v`` bind nothing
        else:
            stack.extend(current.children)
    return names


def module_binding_lines(root: Any, source: bytes, lines: list[str]) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Names bound at module level by assignment, ``def`` or ``class``, each with the
    weight of its binding; imports excluded.

    A data binding weighs its code lines (string lines included: a regex's text is
    what a reader decodes); a ``def`` or ``class`` weighs one line, because a
    caller reads a name and a signature, not a body. A name bound more than once
    (say in both arms of a module-level ``if``) sums its bindings. Descends
    through module-level ``if``/``try``/``with``/loops, never into a function or
    class body.
    """
    bound: dict[str, int] = {}
    stack = list(root.children)
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type in _IMPORTS:
            continue
        if node.type in _DEFINITIONS or node.type in _ASSIGNMENTS:
            size = _binding_size(node, lines)
            for target in _binding_targets(node, source):
                bound[target] = bound.get(target, 0) + size
            continue
        stack.extend(node.children)
    return bound


def _binding_targets(node: Any, source: bytes) -> set[str]:
    """The names a ``def``, ``class`` or assignment binds."""
    if node.type in _DEFINITIONS:
        name = node.child_by_field_name("name")
        return set() if name is None else {_text(name, source)}
    left = node.child_by_field_name("left")
    return set() if left is None else _target_names(left, source)


def _binding_size(node: Any, lines: list[str]) -> int:
    """Code lines of a data binding, string lines included; a ``def``/``class`` weighs one line."""
    if node.type in _DEFINITIONS:
        return 1
    return len(code_lines(lines, node.start_point[0] + 1, node.end_point[0] + 1))


def _bound_in_body(fn_node: Any, source: bytes) -> set[str]:
    """Names the function binds: parameters, assignment targets, loop and ``as`` targets,
    walrus names, comprehension variables, nested ``def``/``class`` names, and its own name."""
    bound: set[str] = set()
    name = fn_node.child_by_field_name("name")
    if name is not None:
        bound.add(_text(name, source))
    params = fn_node.child_by_field_name("parameters")
    if params is not None:
        bound |= _target_names(params, source)
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    stack = [body] if body is not None else []
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type in _ASSIGNMENTS:
            left = node.child_by_field_name("left")
            if left is not None:
                bound |= _target_names(left, source)
        elif node.type in ("for_statement", "for_in_clause"):
            left = node.child_by_field_name("left")
            if left is not None:
                bound |= _target_names(left, source)
        elif node.type == "named_expression":
            left = node.child_by_field_name("name")
            if left is not None:
                bound |= _target_names(left, source)
        elif node.type == "as_pattern":
            alias = node.child_by_field_name("alias")
            if alias is not None:
                bound |= _target_names(alias, source)
        elif node.type in _DEFINITIONS:
            inner = node.child_by_field_name("name")
            if inner is not None:
                bound.add(_text(inner, source))
        stack.extend(node.children)
    return bound


def global_reads(fn_node: Any, source: bytes, module_names: set[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Module-level names (see :func:`module_binding_lines`) the body reads and does not rebind."""
    body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
    if body is None or not module_names:
        return []
    bound = _bound_in_body(fn_node, source)
    reads: set[str] = set()
    stack = [body]
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type == "identifier":
            parent = node.parent
            if parent is not None and (
                (parent.type == "attribute" and parent.child_by_field_name("attribute") == node)
                or (parent.type == "keyword_argument" and parent.child_by_field_name("name") == node)
                or (parent.type in _DEFINITIONS and parent.child_by_field_name("name") == node)
            ):
                continue
            reads.add(_text(node, source))
        stack.extend(node.children)
    return sorted((reads & module_names) - bound)


class DataflowMeasurer:
    """hypergumbo's per-function CFG + reaching definitions, as ``ddg_build`` runs them."""

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        from hypergumbo_core import cfg as cfg_mod
        from hypergumbo_core.dataflow_scope import ensure_def_use_extractors_registered
        from hypergumbo_core.ddg_build import get_ddg_language

        ensure_def_use_extractors_registered()
        self._cfg = cfg_mod
        self._mapping = cfg_mod.load_cfg_mapping("python")
        self._spec = get_ddg_language("python")
        if self._mapping is None or self._spec is None:  # pragma: no cover - shipped
            raise RuntimeError("hypergumbo has no Python cfg mapping or DDG spec")

    def measure(
        self, fn_node: Any, source: bytes, sym_id: str, lines_of_code: set[int],
    ) -> tuple[Optional[int], Optional[int], Optional[int], Optional[int]]:
        """``(distinct edges, distinct variables, reach, uncovered lines)``; ``None``s on bail-out.

        ``uncovered lines`` is ``None`` when the language's coverage gate cannot
        say (no ``call_node_types``), which the caller treats as suspect too.
        """
        body = fn_node.child_by_field_name("body")
        if body is None:
            return 0, 0, 0, 0
        params = self._spec.params_for(fn_node, source) if self._spec.params_for else []
        start_line = fn_node.start_point[0] + 1
        entry_defs = [(p, start_line) for p in params]
        try:
            cfg = self._cfg.build_function_cfg(body, source, self._mapping, sym_id)
            self._cfg.populate_def_use_for_cfg(cfg, body, source, "python")
            result = self._cfg.solve_reaching_defs(cfg, entry_defs)
            if result.bailed_out:
                return None, None, None, None
            edges = list(result.ddg_edges)
            edges.extend(self._cfg.late_capture_edges(cfg, body, self._mapping, edges))
            uncovered = self._cfg.uncovered_semantic_lines(cfg, body, source, self._mapping)
        except Exception:  # pragma: no cover - defensive, mirrors ddg_build
            return None, None, None, None
        triples = {(e.variable, e.def_line, e.use_line) for e in edges}
        variables = {variable for variable, _, _ in triples}
        reach = 0
        for _, def_line, use_line in triples:
            if def_line == start_line:
                continue  # a parameter: the signature shows it
            low, high = sorted((def_line, use_line))
            between = sum(1 for line in lines_of_code if low < line < high)
            reach = max(reach, between)
        return (
            len(triples), len(variables), reach,
            None if uncovered is None else len(uncovered),
        )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scoring

def _analyzer_symbols(path: Path) -> dict[int, Any]:
    """hypergumbo's function/method symbols for the file, by start line."""
    from hypergumbo_lang_mainstream.py import extract_nodes

    result = extract_nodes(path)
    return {
        s.span.start_line: s
        for s in result.symbols
        if s.kind in ("function", "method") and s.span is not None
    }


def flag(score: FunctionScore, thresholds: Thresholds) -> None:
    """Set ``flags`` (each ``measure value>threshold``) and the band."""
    flags: list[str] = []
    if score.loc > thresholds.loc:
        flags.append(f"loc {score.loc}>{thresholds.loc}")
    if score.mccabe is not None and score.mccabe > thresholds.mccabe:
        flags.append(f"mccabe {score.mccabe}>{thresholds.mccabe}")
    if score.cognitive > thresholds.cognitive:
        flags.append(f"cognitive {score.cognitive}>{thresholds.cognitive}")
    if score.nesting > thresholds.nesting:
        flags.append(f"nesting {score.nesting}>{thresholds.nesting}")
    if (thresholds.dataflow is not None and score.dataflow is not None
            and score.dataflow > thresholds.dataflow):
        flags.append(f"dataflow {score.dataflow}>{thresholds.dataflow}")
    score.flags = flags
    score.band = "ok" if not flags else ("watch" if len(flags) == 1 else "split")


def score_file(path: Path, thresholds: Thresholds, *, dataflow: bool = True) -> FileScore:
    source = path.read_bytes()
    lines = source.decode("utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines()
    root = _parse(source)
    symbols = _analyzer_symbols(path)
    measurer = DataflowMeasurer() if dataflow else None
    binding_lines = module_binding_lines(root, source, lines)
    module_names = set(binding_lines)
    skipped = non_logical_lines(source)

    scores: list[FunctionScore] = []
    definition_spans: list[tuple[int, int]] = []
    for node in _function_nodes(root):
        start = node.start_point[0] + 1
        end = node.end_point[0] + 1
        definition_spans.append((start, end))
        name = _qualified_name(node, source)
        symbol = symbols.get(start)
        sym_id = symbol.id if symbol is not None else f"python:{path}:{start}-{end}:{name}:function"
        params: Optional[int] = None
        if symbol is not None and symbol.meta and "parameters" in symbol.meta:
            params = len(symbol.meta["parameters"])
        doc_span = _docstring_span(node)
        own_lines = code_lines(lines, start, end, doc_span)
        edges = variables = reach = uncovered = None
        if measurer is not None:
            edges, variables, reach, uncovered = measurer.measure(node, source, sym_id, own_lines)
        names = global_reads(node, source, module_names)
        score = FunctionScore(
            name=name, start_line=start, end_line=end,
            loc=len(own_lines - skipped), loc_physical=len(own_lines),
            doc=0 if doc_span is None else doc_span[1] - doc_span[0] + 1,
            mccabe=symbol.cyclomatic_complexity if symbol is not None else None,
            cognitive=cognitive_complexity(node, source),
            nesting=max_nesting(node),
            dataflow=edges, dataflow_uncovered=uncovered, vars=variables, reach=reach,
            globals=len(names), global_names=names,
            global_lines=sum(binding_lines[n] for n in names),
            params=params, calls=count_calls(node),
        )
        flag(score, thresholds)
        scores.append(score)

    # Class bodies are not functions, but their lines are not module code either.
    stack = [root]
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node.type == "class_definition":
            definition_spans.append((node.start_point[0] + 1, node.end_point[0] + 1))
        stack.extend(node.children)
    all_code = code_lines(lines, 1, len(lines))
    module_lines = sum(
        1 for line in all_code
        if not any(start <= line <= end for start, end in definition_spans)
    )
    summary = _summary(scores)
    summary["module_lines"] = module_lines
    summary["module_bindings"] = len(module_names)
    return FileScore(path=str(path), lines=len(lines), functions=scores, summary=summary)


def _summary(scores: list[FunctionScore]) -> dict[str, Any]:
    def column(attr: str) -> list[int]:
        return [v for v in (getattr(s, attr) for s in scores) if v is not None]

    out: dict[str, Any] = {
        "functions": len(scores),
        "bands": {b: sum(1 for s in scores if s.band == b) for b in ("ok", "watch", "split")},
        "flag_counts": {},
        "dataflow_suspect": sum(1 for s in scores if s.dataflow_suspect),
    }
    for s in scores:
        for f in s.flags:
            key = f.split(" ")[0]
            out["flag_counts"][key] = out["flag_counts"].get(key, 0) + 1
    for attr in (
        "loc", "mccabe", "cognitive", "nesting", "dataflow", "reach", "globals", "global_lines",
    ):
        values = column(attr)
        out[attr] = {
            "max": max(values) if values else None,
            "median": statistics.median(values) if values else None,
        }
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- output

def _sorted(scores: list[FunctionScore], key: str) -> list[FunctionScore]:
    if key == "name":
        return sorted(scores, key=lambda s: s.name)
    if key == "line":
        return sorted(scores, key=lambda s: s.start_line)
    attr = "global_lines" if key == "globals" else key  # the text column, not the count
    return sorted(
        scores,
        key=lambda s: (-(getattr(s, attr) or 0), -s.cognitive, -s.loc, s.start_line),
    )


def _cell(value: Optional[int]) -> str:
    return "-" if value is None else str(value)


def _dataflow_cell(score: FunctionScore) -> str:
    if score.dataflow is None:
        return "-"
    return f"{score.dataflow}?" if score.dataflow_suspect else str(score.dataflow)


def render_text(report: FileScore, sort: str, top: Optional[int]) -> str:
    rows = _sorted(report.functions, sort)
    if top is not None:
        rows = rows[:top]
    width = max((len(s.name) for s in rows), default=4)
    header = (f"{'band':5} {'function':{width}} {'lines':>11} {'loc':>4} {'doc':>3} {'mcc':>4} "
              f"{'cog':>4} {'nest':>4} {'dflow':>6} {'vars':>4} {'reach':>5} {'gloc':>4}  flags")
    out = [f"{report.path}: {report.lines} lines, {len(report.functions)} functions", header]
    for s in rows:
        out.append(
            f"{s.band:5} {s.name:{width}} {s.start_line:>5}-{s.end_line:<5} {s.loc:>4} {s.doc:>3} "
            f"{_cell(s.mccabe):>4} {s.cognitive:>4} {s.nesting:>4} {_dataflow_cell(s):>6} "
            f"{_cell(s.vars):>4} {_cell(s.reach):>5} {s.global_lines:>4}  {', '.join(s.flags)}"
        )
    sm = report.summary
    suspect = f"; dataflow suspect {sm['dataflow_suspect']}" if sm["dataflow_suspect"] else ""
    out.append(
        f"summary: ok {sm['bands']['ok']}, watch {sm['bands']['watch']}, split {sm['bands']['split']}"
        f"{suspect}; module lines {sm['module_lines']}, module bindings {sm['module_bindings']}; "
        + "; ".join(
            f"{label} max {sm[k]['max']} median {sm[k]['median']}"
            for k, label in (
                ("loc", "loc"), ("mccabe", "mccabe"), ("cognitive", "cognitive"),
                ("nesting", "nesting"), ("dataflow", "dataflow"), ("global_lines", "global lines"),
            )
            if sm[k]["max"] is not None
        )
    )
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv: Optional[list[str]] = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="score-readability",
        description="Per-function reader-load panel for Python files (see the module docstring).",
    )
    parser.add_argument("files", nargs="+", type=Path)
    parser.add_argument("--sort", choices=_SORT_KEYS, default="cognitive")
    parser.add_argument("--top", type=int, default=None, help="show only the first N rows per file")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="emit the full panel as JSON")
    parser.add_argument("--strict", action="store_true",
                        help="exit 1 when any function exceeds a threshold")
    parser.add_argument("--no-dataflow", action="store_true",
                        help="skip the DDG measure (faster on very large files)")
    parser.add_argument("--max-loc", type=int, default=Thresholds.loc)
    parser.add_argument("--max-mccabe", type=int, default=Thresholds.mccabe)
    parser.add_argument("--max-cognitive", type=int, default=Thresholds.cognitive)
    parser.add_argument("--max-nesting", type=int, default=Thresholds.nesting)
    parser.add_argument("--max-dataflow", type=int, default=None,
                        help="no default: no widely recognised threshold exists")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    thresholds = Thresholds(
        loc=args.max_loc, mccabe=args.max_mccabe, cognitive=args.max_cognitive,
        nesting=args.max_nesting, dataflow=args.max_dataflow,
    )
    reports: list[FileScore] = []
    for path in args.files:
        if not path.is_file():
            print(f"score-readability: not a file: {path}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        reports.append(score_file(path, thresholds, dataflow=not args.no_dataflow))

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps({
            "thresholds": asdict(thresholds),
            "files": [asdict(r) for r in reports],
        }, indent=2))
    else:
        print("\n\n".join(render_text(r, args.sort, args.top) for r in reports))

    if args.strict and any(s.band != "ok" for r in reports for s in r.functions):
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
